{
  "id": "question-powers/v1",
  "about": "Which level's own law answers each policy question, and which dial of the powers catalog (catalog.json) the answer turns. A level answers a question only when the question is its own (levels) and the catalog does not say no for that dial at that level. \"state\" also covers D.C. and the territories, which answer through their own catalog columns; the D.C. Council also holds the District's city and county powers, and a consolidated city-county holds both. A catalog cell that is UNKNOWN or varies by state neither permits nor refuses (Decision Register, Sept. 22): the level may act and its authority stays unsettled until researched. why says why a question is narrower than its issue's levels, or why federal law answers a state question. Read by src/simulation/governing/question-authority.ts.",
  "questions": {
    "us-policy-positions:fiscal.adopt-income-tax": {
      "dial": "income-tax",
      "levels": ["state"],
      "why": "It asks whether the state levies an income tax. A local income tax is levied under state law, and is not this question."
    },
    "us-policy-positions:fiscal.graduated-income-tax": {
      "dial": "income-tax",
      "levels": ["state"],
      "why": "It asks about the state's own personal income tax. Where a city or county taxes income, state law sets that tax's rates."
    },
    "us-policy-positions:fiscal.cap-property-tax-growth": {
      "dial": "property-tax",
      "levels": ["state"],
      "why": "A property tax bill adds up every local levy on a home, so only state law can cap how fast the bill grows. A local government setting its own levy is a budget decision."
    },
